I think you can achieve this by using `torch.nn.ModuleList` and `nn.Linear`. Here is an example:

```
import torch
from torch import nn

class MyModel(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.layers = nn.ModuleList([nn.Linear(10, 5), nn.Linear(10, 3), nn.Linear(10, 2)])
        self.out = nn.Linear(10, 1)

    def forward(self, x):
        for i, l in enumerate(self.layers):
            if i == 0:
                out = l(x)
            elif i == 1:
                out += l(x[:, [0, 1]])
            else:
                out += l(x[:, [0, 1, 2]])

        return self.out(out)
```

The problem here is that you're passing a `std::vector` instead of a `torch::Tensor`. You can convert your `std::vector` to a `torch::Tensor` using [`torch::from_blob`](https://pytorch.org/cppdocs/api/generated/torch.from_blob.html#torch-from-blob):

```
// Create a vector of inputs.
std::vector<float> row = {0.160625, 0.967468297, 3.520480583, 0.862454481, -0.341933766};
std::vector<torch::Tensor> inputs;

inputs.emplace_back(
    torch::from_blob(
        row.data(),
        {1, static_cast<int>(row.size())},
        torch::kFloat)
);

// Execute the model and turn its output into a tensor.
at::Tensor output = module.forward(inputs).toTensor();
std::cout << output.slice(/*dim=*/1, /*start=*/0, /*end=*/4) << '\n';
```

