I'm not familiar with this term but I think you're referring to [this](http://www.jmlr.org/papers/volume15/srivastava14a/srivastava14a.pdf) paper.

Here's an implementation that should work:

```
import torch.nn as nn

class CurDrop(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, p=0.5):
        super(CurDrop, self).__init__()
        self.p = p

    def forward(self, x):
        if self.training:
            mask = (x.data.new(x.size()).bernoulli_(self.p)).to(dtype=x.dtype)
            return x * mask / self.p
        else:
            return x
```

The problem is that you're trying to animate an `img` element which doesn't have any position set (so it defaults to `static`). You need to set its position to either `relative`, `absolute` or `fixed`. In your case, since you want it to stay within the bounds of the parent container, `position: relative` should work.

Here's a working example:

```html
<div id="game">
  <img id="asd" src="http://i.imgur.com/ApuSWMC.png" alt="torch" style="position: relative;">
</div>
```

```javascript
$('#asd').click(function () {
  $(this).animate({
    left: "+=100px"
  }, 'fast');
});
```

Note that I've added `style="position: relative;"` directly to the `img` tag for demonstration purposes. You can also add it via CSS if you prefer.
